From 83864afcee4337b21682513f5efe2fe670802624 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 08:27:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] feat(registry): enter
 basic/utilities/{ping,progress,cancellation} at 2025-11-25

The three pages sat in `out_of_scope` as an "unclaimed surface, wire-checkable,
expansion candidate", so a server sending progress for a token nobody issued, or
a cancellation naming a request nobody sent, judged clean at this revision while
the same traffic failed at 2026-07-28. They now enter by the registry's own
method: every MUST-family and SHOULD-family keyword instance on each page is
entered (27 entries: 13 judged by 11 checks, 14 excluded with a reason that says
what a recording would have to carry and why it does not), the MAY-family ones
counted. The census: ping 1/6/2, progress 8/3/3, cancellation 4/5/1.

Ids are new (PING-001..007, PROG-009..019, CANC-018..026): an id names one
clause at one revision, forever, and ids are unique across the whole set, so a
sentence 2026-07-28 also carries (PROG-001/PROG-009, PROG-005/PROG-013, ...)
gets a second entry under a second id, as BASE-001/BASE-043 do. The continuity
gate keys on page section as well as quote, so none of the pairs is compared;
each 2025-11-25 entry binds `both` where 2026-07-28's binds one party, because
either party issues requests at this revision. CANC-018 is judged where its
2026-07-28 twin (CANC-010) is excluded, on purpose: a 2025-11-25 session begins
with `initialize`, so a recording that holds the handshake holds every request
the session issued, the witness the stateless revision lacks (PAGE-003/PAGE-011
is the precedent).

The core embeds the three area files after the transport ones; its two pinned
entry counts move from 321 to 348, the area-order test learns the three
prefixes, and xtask's pinned in-scope page count moves from 13 to 16.

